{"type": "plan", "agent": "lead", "reasoning": "Worst case for REQ-006 is 2750 N with thickness reduced by 0.5 mm (h_eff = h - 0.5). Bending stress is 6PL/(b h^2) with PL = 385,000 N-mm at the worst load. Tip deflection is 4PL^3/(E b h^3) <= 3 mm. Allowables at 1.25 yield and 1.6 ultimate: Al 193.75 MPa (ultimate governs), Ti 593.75 MPa (ultimate governs), Steel 348 MPa (yield governs). Each material's optimum sits where the stress, stiffness, and h/b <= 2 limits meet at the smallest section area. Approximate results: Ti h=20, b=12 (240 mm2, about 0.149 kg); Al h=30, b=15 (450 mm2, about 0.170 kg); Steel h=25, b=13 (325 mm2, about 0.357 kg). Titanium is the lightest candidate, so the team should verify it first.", "notes": {"Al 6061-T6": "Ultimate allowable 193.75 MPa governs at 2750 N with h_eff = h - 0.5, so search thick plates near h=25-30 mm with b=15-20 mm, where h/b <= 2 binds. Expected best is about h=30, b=15 (450 mm2, about 0.170 kg), since b=14 violates the ratio limit.", "Ti-6Al-4V": "Ultimate allowable 593.75 MPa leaves stress with large margin, so tip stiffness (E=113.8 GPa) is the binding limit. Search h=16-25 mm with b=12-24 mm, centered on h=20, b=12 (240 mm2, about 0.149 kg); verify deflection margin there is only about 0.6%.", "Steel 4130": "Yield allowable 348 MPa governs and the high modulus (205 GPa) gives deflection margin, but density 7850 kg/m3 penalizes mass. Search h=20-25 mm with b=13-18 mm, best candidate h=25, b=13 (325 mm2, about 0.357 kg), which is the heaviest of the three."}, "t": 25.4}
